        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"center\":5,\"craft\":4,\"facts\":4,\"age_fit\":5,\"continuity\":4,\"delight\":4},\"evidence\":{\"center\":\"Lennon is the only child and acts in every level: he volleys through the cuju hole, dribbles 'past three farmers and a very confused goose', runs the 1863 vote ('No kicking people in the shins?'), takes the first penalty, grabs the flickering trophy, unwraps Pickles' bundle and scores the final top-corner penalty. His penalty practice and his shootout game shape both the McCrum scene and the finale. His weakest level is 1950, where 'Not on my watch' leads to nothing he actually does.\",\"craft\":\"There is a strong cold open at the backyard fence, a clear device (the glowing ball and the fence opening into a portal), a return home with a keepsake message, and a finale that calls back every level. However, Level 6 has no challenge or flicker, and the coin drops with Lennon only watching. The last line is also duplicated: 'Saturday couldn't come soon enough.' appears in Full Time and again after 'The End', which blunts the landing.\",\"facts\":\"The facts are broadly accurate and woven into action: the Freemasons' Tavern in 1863, Oxford-style 'soccer' from 'association', the different ball in each half with Argentina 2–1 at half-time, the Gaetjens header, Pickles, Ken Aston's traffic lights and the Dick, Kerr crowds with the 50-year ban. Two slips: the 'very first penalty spot in history' is anachronistic (in 1891 kicks were taken from a 12-yard line, and the spot came later), and the England newspapers 'typo' tale is a popular legend stated without a hedge.\",\"age_fit\":\"At 2,633 words it is comfortably within 2,200–3,200. The wit suits a 10-year-old ('That's the most sensible silly idea I've ever heard', 'Okay, that one doesn't count,' he told nobody). Peril is moderate and brief (the mob stampede, the static goalkeeper), and nobody is hurt.\",\"continuity\":\"Bit, the golden coins fusing into a trophy, the watery pixel eyes, 